<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Inner Austria</h1><p class="st-meta">1379 CE – 1619 CE · capital Graz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Habsburg lands of Styria, Carinthia and Carniola, ruled from Graz, from which Ferdinand II launched the Counter-Reformation across the monarchy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuberg">Treaty of Neuberg</a> of 1379 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria">Leopold III</a> the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> lands south and west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. These duchies were ruled as Inner Austria by Leopold's descendants in the 15th century; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Frederick III</a>, who resided at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt">Wiener Neustadt</a>, later reunited the dynasty's lands. In 1564 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand I</a>'s will recreated Inner Austria for his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-archduke-of-austria">Charles II</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> the archdukes governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> with their own estates, which were largely Protestant in the 16th century. Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> bore the brunt of Ottoman raids and paid for the Croatian and Slavonian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/military-frontier">Military Frontier</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-archduke-of-austria">Charles II</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-graz">University of Graz</a> in 1585 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, and his son Ferdinand expelled Protestant preachers from 1598.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1619 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Archduke Ferdinand</a> became emperor as Ferdinand II, and his Inner Austrian lands merged into the united <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy</a>, though separate institutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> survived until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa's</a> reforms in the 1740s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/inner-austria.jpg" alt="Inner Austria" width="320" height="276"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Carl Kindermann, gestochen von Christoph Junker · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AvI_Provinz_Inner%C3%B6sterreich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1379 CE – 1619 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Graz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ernest the Iron, Charles II, Ferdinand II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Slovene</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Innerösterreich</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1499&amp;focus=inner-austria" aria-label="Inner Austria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5022 537 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Inner Austria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5342 793L5381 747M5342 793L5381 747"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 747h0M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5342 793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5342 793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1499&amp;focus=inner-austria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
